  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186087722,
      "id": "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef",
      "title": "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "44951240-82b0-5104-98c5-37239eefccda",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} + 2 t - 1,\\quad y=- 2 t^{2} + 4 t - 3,\\quad t\\le 1 ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=- t^{2} + 2 t - 1 \\\\ &=- \\left(t - 1\\right)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter $t=1$ is the domain boundary, which is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $x\\le 0$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &=- 2 t^{2} + 4 t - 3 \\\\ &=- 2 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter $t=1$ is the domain boundary, which is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $y\\le -1$.\n\nTherefore, $x\\le 0,\\,y\\le -1$.",
